That gives a fully-observed total of $130M in assessed value uplift already showing up in the 2026 assessment from the 2024 cohort alone. It understates the eventual value of that cohort: the "not yet fully reflected" group is likely still a stale, mid-construction assessment rather than the finished-building value — exactly the gap the model-based extrapolation later in this post is designed to estimate.

#### From gross uplift to a fairer comparison

The raw 2024-to-2026 difference in assessed value overstates the effect of redevelopment on its own, because Edmonton property values were also generally rising over that period. To isolate the redevelopment effect, I build a comparison: what would each property have been worth in 2026 if it hadn't been redeveloped? I estimate this from a group of RS-zoned properties in mature neighbourhoods that were not part of any 2024-2026 residential new-build permit, taking the median ratio of their 2026 to 2024 assessed value as a general appreciation factor. This un-redeveloped control group may not appreciate at exactly the rate the redeveloped lots would have, but it's a reasonable counterfactual, and good enough for what I'm trying to measure here.


::: {.cell}

:::


Across 81,753 non-redeveloped RS Zone properties in mature neighbourhoods, the median 2024-to-2026 growth ratio is 1.19x, meaning a typical mature-neighbourhood house that wasn't touched still gained about 19% in assessed value over those two years. I use this as the baseline against which redevelopment uplift is measured. Net uplift is the completed 5 to 8 unit rowhome's 2026 value minus 1.19 times its 2024 baseline, isolating the redevelopment effect from general market appreciation. Gross uplift is the simple, unadjusted difference, shown alongside it for comparison.

@fig-uplift-distribution shows the distribution of both measures across the 48 matched properties, split by project size.


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Distribution of assessed value uplift per redeveloped property, gross and net of market appreciation](index_files/figure-html/fig-uplift-distribution-1.png){#fig-uplift-distribution width=672}
:::
:::


@tbl-uplift-rules compares three simple ways of summarizing this uplift into a single per-project figure: a median per property within two size buckets (5-6 units and 7-8 units), a single median per property across all sizes, and a median per home added, which can be scaled by any project's unit count. These serve as sanity anchors for the model-based rule developed next.


::: {#tbl-uplift-rules .cell tbl-cap='Three ways to summarize per-project uplift for 5 to 8 unit rowhomes, gross and net of appreciation'}
::: {.cell-output-display}

