## Conclusions

The RS Zone's small-scale infill isn't just adding homes, it's rebuilding the property tax base of neighbourhoods that had been losing population and, with it, tax revenue per hectare of city-maintained infrastructure for decades. Across 5 to 8 unit rowhomes, 2 to 4 unit plexes, and backyard homes, a handful of redevelopments per neighbourhood, concentrated in mature areas that already have the pipes, roads, and schools in place, is already worth more than $14.9M a year in additional municipal property tax capacity at steady state, on top of the housing it delivers. The 5 to 8 unit rowhome remains the single largest lever on its own, worth more than $10.8M a year of that total — but it's not the whole story.

None of this required a subsidy, a grant, or a City-led development. It required removing rules that made this kind of building illegal. If you'd like to help keep it that way, consider joining [Grow Together Edmonton](https://www.growtogetheryeg.com/).
